# Activation and first run (AC)

This is the short form. Before writing code in this area, read the full rules with reasons, bad/good examples and checks: `.ui-polish/rules/26-activation.md`.

- **AC-01 The first screen is designed for a new account** (error). Design the first screen a brand-new user sees as its own state, not the full dashboard with zeros. It names the one most valuable first action ("Create your first invoice") as the primary button, offers a faster alternative where there is one (import from CSV or another tool, start from a template, explore with sample data), and hides charts, KPIs and tables that have nothing to show yet. Once the user has real data, the normal screen takes over.
- **AC-02 Reach the first success fast** (warning). Identify the activation moment for the product (write it in `design.md`) and remove everything between signup and that moment that is not strictly needed: defer profile, team, billing and preference questions until after it; pre-fill with defaults (UX-11); let users try with sample data before connecting their own; keep any required setup to three steps or fewer with visible progress (UX-09). Celebrate the moment with a clear confirmation and the next step (UX-12), not confetti alone.
- **AC-03 A setup checklist for the rest** (warning). Put deferred setup into a short checklist (three to six items) on the home screen: each item says the benefit ("Connect your bank so payments mark invoices paid"), links straight to the place to do it, ticks itself when done (detected, not self-reported), and shows progress ("2 of 5 done", UX-09). Order items by value. The checklist can be dismissed and stays dismissed; it disappears by itself once complete, after confirming completion.
- **AC-04 Empty states show the way forward** (warning). Every list, table, board and chart has a designed empty state (ST-03) that distinguishes "nothing yet" from "nothing matches your filters" and "you don't have access". The first kind explains what will appear here and why it is useful, in one sentence, with a primary action to create the first item and, where useful, import or a sample. The second offers to clear the filters. Use a small, on-brand illustration or icon, never a large stock image.
- **AC-05 Help in context, not tours** (warning). Prefer contextual help to product tours: an inline hint next to a feature the first time it is relevant, a short description in the empty state, a "?" link to the right help article. If a tour is genuinely needed, keep it to three steps or fewer, start it only on request or on first visit to that screen, make it skippable at every step and never show it again once dismissed (remember this per user, on the server). Never block the interface with a modal tour on first login.
- **AC-06 Invite and share when there is something to share** (info). Ask users to invite teammates or share their work after they have created something worth sharing, not during signup: offer "Invite your accountant" after the first invoice, "Share this report" when a report is built. Make the invite form accept several emails at once, show pending invites with resend and revoke actions, and let invitees land straight on the shared item with context about who invited them.
- **AC-07 Returning users land where they left off** (info). After the first run, the home screen serves returning users: what needs attention now (overdue items, mentions, failed jobs), recently opened items, and the most frequent action. Restore the last workspace, filters and view (NV-06). When something changed since the last visit, say what ("3 invoices were paid since Monday"). Do not show onboarding content to users who have finished it.
